, W3, and W6 is separate.\\n- T1, T2, T4: could form chows? T1,T2,T3 or T2,T3,T4 or T4,T5,T6. But I have T1, T2, T2, T4. So possible chow: T1,T2,T3 (need T3) or T2,T3,T4 (need T3) or T4,T5,T6 (need T5,T6).\\n- B6 is isolated.\\n\\nSo my hand is fragmented. I need to discard a tile that is least useful for forming melds.\\n\\nAlso consider scoring patterns: I have an exposed pung of F3 (West Wind). Prevailing wind is West Wind (F3), so that pung gives Prevalent Wind (2 points). Seat wind is South Wind (F2), not in my hand. Also, I have no dragons yet.\\n\\nPotential discards: W6, T1, W3, B6, T4, T2, W2.\\n\\nI should consider tile safety based on discards and melds of others. Look at game history:\\n\\nPrevious player (to my left) has melds: [[T3,T3,T3],[T6,T6,T6]]. They have pungs of T3 and T6. Their discards include: J2, F4, B7, F4, B9, W4, (Peng T3), B2, F4, (Peng T6), B4, W1, W4, W2, B1, B5. So they discarded W2 and B1 recently. They might be safe for those.\\n\\nNext player has melds: [[T9,T9,T9],[W4,W5,W6]]. Their discards: B8, W8, J3, B2, (Peng T9), T2, (Chi W5), T1, B1, T8, T5, T7, T8. They discarded T1, T2, T5, T7, T8. So T1 and T2 are somewhat safe.\\n\\nAcross player has meld: [[B4,B4,B4]]. Discards: F3, W1, F2, J3, B8, B8, F3, (Peng B4), T8, B7, W9, B2, J1.\\n\\nUnshown tiles count: Important to see what tiles are left. For example, W4: 0 left (all used), T3: 1 left, T6: 1 left, etc.\\n\\nNow, which discard improves my hand toward potential winning patterns? I have many terminals and simples. I have no honors in hand except the F3 meld. So I could aim for All Simples (2 points) if I discard terminals/honors. But I already have an honor meld (F3), so All Simples is impossible because honor tiles are present. Actually, All Simples requires no terminals or honors in the hand. My hand has T1 (terminal), and meld has F3 (honor), so All Simples is out.\\n\\nMaybe aim for Mixed Triple Chow? Not likely.\\n\\nBetter to think about efficiency: I need two more melds