d one pung. If I form two more chows, I could have All Chows (2 points). But I have a pung, so not All Chows. Actually, All Chows requires four chows and a pair. I have one pung, so not eligible.\\n\\nMaybe aim for Half Flush? I have honor tiles (F3) and suited tiles of one suit? My suited tiles are W, T, B mixed. Not likely.\\n\\nSo just try to win with basic patterns: Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point for F3), Prevalent Wind (2 points), maybe Self Drawn (1 point), etc. That's only 4 points, not enough for 8 points minimum. I need at least 8 points to win. So I must aim for more points.\\n\\nI need to consider special patterns. With my current hand, it's difficult. Maybe I can try to form a Kong? Unlikely.\\n\\nPerhaps I should consider discarding a tile that might lead to a hand with more points. But I'm far from winning.\\n\\nMaybe I should play defensively because others might be close to winning. Look at melds: Next player has T9 pung and W4-W6 chow. They might be going for All Pungs? Or something. Across player has B4 pung. Previous player has T3 and T6 pungs. They might be collecting pungs. So many pungs, meaning they might be close to winning.\\n\\nI should discard safe tiles. Look at tiles that have been discarded multiple times:\\n\\n- W2: discarded by previous player.\\n- T1: discarded by next player.\\n- T2: discarded by next player.\\n- B6: claimed by me, so it was discarded by previous player? Actually, \\\"Chi B6\\\" means I took a B6 from discard. Whose discard? Likely previous player's discard because Chi only from left player. In history, previous player's last discards: ... Play B5. So before that, they played B1. So B6 was not discarded? Wait, \\\"Chi B6\\\" is my action. The tile I claimed was B6. It must have been discarded by previous player. But in previous player's history, the last action is \\\"Play B5\\\". So B6 discard is not listed? Possibly it was the discard before my Chi. Actually, game history might not list every discard? It says \\\"Your game history is: ... Chi B6.\\\" So my last action was Chi B6. That means previous player discarded B6, and I claimed it. But in previous player's history, it ends with \\\"Play B5\\\". So maybe B6 discard is not recorded in their history? Or maybe I misread. Anyway, B6 is recently used.\\n\\nSafe discards: T1 seems safe because next player discarded it. W2 seems safe because previous player discarded it. B6 might be safe because it was just used.\\n\\nBut also consider that if I discard a tile that completes someone's hand, I lose. So I need to avoid dangerous tiles.\\n\\nMaybe discard T1 because it's terminal and next player discarded it. But terminals can be dangerous for those collecting terminals.\\n\\nBetter to discard a tile that is not needed for common winning hands. Since many players have pungs, they might be waiting for pungs or honors.\\n\\nI have W6, which is in next player's chow, so they probably don't need another W6. But they might need it for a pair? Unlikely.\\n\\nI think T1 is relatively safe.\\n\\nBut let's evaluate a few promising moves:\\n\\n1. Play T1: Discard terminal T1. It breaks the T1-T2 sequence, but I still have T2 pair and T4. T1 is not useful for chow without T3. And T3 is rare (1 left). So T1 is not very useful. Safety: next player discarded T1, so might be safe.\\n\\n2. Play B6: Discard simple B6. It's isolated. Safety: B6 was recently claimed, so might be safe.\\n\\n3. Play W6: Discard simple W6. It's isolated. Safety: W6 is in next player's meld, so they might not need it.\\n\\n4. Play T4: Discard simple T4. It could be used for chow with T3,T5, but T3 is rare. Safety: T4 hasn't been discarded much? Across player discarded none? Actually, T4 not in discards. So might be dangerous.\\n\\n5. Play W2 or W3: These connect, so discarding one breaks a potential chow. But if I discard W2, I keep W3 which can pair with another W3? Unshown W3: 4 left. But I have only one W3. So better to keep the sequence.\\n\\nI think T1 and B6 are the least useful.\\n\\nNow, for scoring, I need to think about future possibilities. If I keep W2-W3, I can hope for W1 or W4 to complete chow. Unshown W1: 1 left, W4: 0 left. So W4 is gone, W1 is 1 left. So chance for W1 chow is low. So W2-W3 might not be useful. Actually, W2-W3 can also become a pair if I get another W2 or W3. Unshown W2: 3 left, W3: 4 left. So possible.\\n\\nMaybe discard W6 because it's not connecting.\\n\\nBut let's see valid actions: Play W6; Play T1; Play W3; Play B6; Play T4; Play T2; Play W2.\\n\\nI should not discard T2 because it's my only pair.\\n\\nSo between W6, T1, W3, B6, T4, W2.\\n\\nI'll choose T1 because it's terminal and seems safe.\\n\\nBut let's check if there's any pattern I could aim for. With my melds: F3 pung and B5-B7 chow. If I can form two more chows with simples, I might get All Simples? But F3 is honor, so not All Simples. Maybe No Honors? But F3 is honor, so not.\\n\\nPerhaps I can aim for Outside Hand? That requires terminals/honors in every meld and pair. My F3 pung qualifies, but my B5-B7 chow does not (all simples). So not.\\n\\nSo just basic win: need at least 8 points. Currently, I have Prevalent Wind (2) + Pung of Terminals or Honors (1) = 3 points. Need 5 more. Could come from Self Drawn (1), Concealed Hand (if I win by discard? but my melds are exposed, so not), All Chows? no, etc. So I need more patterns like Two Dragon Pungs (6) but I have no dragons. So difficult.\\n\\nMaybe I should just play defensively and avoid giving others winning tile.\\n\\nI'll discard T1.\\n\\nReason: T1 is a terminal tile that has been discarded by the next player, making it relatively safe. It is not useful for forming chows in my current hand due to the lack of T3, and discarding it preserves my potential sequences with W2-W3 and the T2 pair.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T1\"}"}
